Day 36: Expense Tracker That Saves to a File

Concepts used: file handling with open(), with, write/append/read modes, try/except

Why files?
Every program you've written so far forgets everything the moment it closes. Your contact book, cart, and inventory all disappear, because they only lived in variables. A file stays on your computer after the program ends. From today, your programs can have memory.

The basics of file handling
Writing a line to a file (append mode):
with open("expenses.txt", "a") as file:
    file.write("Tea,20\n")

Reading all lines from a file:
with open("expenses.txt", "r") as file:
    for line in file:
        print(line.strip())

Here is what each part means:
1.open("expenses.txt", "a") opens the file. The second part is the mode)
    i."a" = append: add to the end, keep what's already there (creates the file if it doesn't exist)
   ii."r" = read: only read it (crashes if the file doesn't exist)
  iii."w" = write: erase everything and start fresh. Be careful with this one.
2.with ... as file: opens the file and closes it automatically when the indented block ends. Always use with so you don't forget to close it.
3.file.write("Tea,20\n") writes text. The \n means "new line". Without it, everything would be squashed onto one line, because write() doesn't add a line break like print() does.
4.line.strip() removes the \n that comes at the end of every line you read.

Think of it like a notebook. "a" means writing on the next empty line. "r" means just reading it. "w" means tearing out all the pages first.
Problem Statement
Build an expense tracker where every expense is saved in a file called expenses.txt. Each line holds one expense in the format description,amount, for example Tea,20. Menu:

1. Add Expense
2. View All Expenses
3. Total Spent
4. Exit

Example Run
Choose: 1
Enter description: Tea
Enter amount: 20
Expense saved!

Choose: 1
Enter description: Bus ticket
Enter amount: 35
Expense saved!

Choose: 2
1. Tea - ₹20.0
2. Bus ticket - ₹35.0

Choose: 3
Total spent: ₹55.0

Choose: 4
Bye

Then close the program and run it again and choose option 2. Your expenses should still be there. That's the whole point of today.
Hints
Add Expense: ask for both values, then append one line:
with open("expenses.txt", "a") as file:
    file.write(f"{description},{amount}\n")

View and Total: read each line and split it with .split(",") (you used this on Day 24):
with open("expenses.txt", "r") as file:
    for line in file:
        description, amount = line.strip().split(",")
        # amount is still text here, so convert it with float(amount)

Total is the same loop, but you add each float(amount) to a running total instead of printing.
Things to handle
The file doesn't exist yet. The very first time someone chooses View or Total, open(..., "r") crashes with FileNotFoundError. Wrap the reading code in try/except FileNotFoundError and print "No expenses yet."
An amount that isn't a number. Convert with float() before saving, so bad input never gets into the file. Reject negative amounts too.
Commas in the description. If someone types Tea, biscuits as the description, the extra comma breaks the description, amount split on reading. A simple fix is to replace commas in the description with spaces before saving: description.replace(",", " ").

Where is the file?
Python creates expenses.txt in the folder you run the program from. You're in ~/daily-python-projects/day36, so run it from there and the file will appear next to your .py file. You can open it any time to see your data:
cat expenses.txt

